from physics_helpers import q,write_bank
items=[
q(1,'What is power?','The rate of energy transfer or the rate of doing work.','4.1.1.4'),
q(1,'What is the SI unit of energy?','Joule (J).','4.1.1.2'),
q(1,'Name the store that increases when a spring is stretched.','Elastic potential.','4.1.1.2'),
q(1,'What does a renewable energy resource mean?','A resource that is replenished as it is used.','4.1.3'),
q(2,'Describe the main energy changes when a ball rises after being thrown, including air resistance.','- Its **kinetic store decreases** while its **gravitational potential store increases**.\n- Air resistance transfers some energy to **thermal stores of the ball and surroundings**.','4.1.1.1','2023 1H Q07.2: related store-transfer reasoning'),
q(2,'A 500 W motor runs for 20 s. Calculate the energy transferred.','**E = Pt** = 500 × 20 = **10,000 J**.','4.1.1.4'),
q(2,'A device receives 800 J and transfers 600 J usefully. Calculate its percentage efficiency.','Efficiency = 600/800 = 0.75. Percentage efficiency = **75%**.','4.1.2.2','2022 1H Q03.3–03.4; 2023 1H Q01.4'),
q(2,'Why is energy described as dissipated rather than destroyed when a moving car brakes?','- Energy transfers from the car’s kinetic store to **thermal stores**.\n- It spreads into the surroundings and becomes less useful, but **total energy is conserved**.','4.1.2.1'),
q(2,'State two control variables when comparing different insulating materials around identical containers of hot water.','- Same **thickness of insulation**.\n- Same **mass of water and starting temperature**.','4.1.2.1','2022 1H Q04.1–04.5'),
q(2,'Explain why a thicker wall of the same material usually reduces cooling, for the same area and temperature difference.','- It increases the distance over which energy must transfer by **conduction**.\n- The **rate of energy transfer decreases**, so the building cools more slowly.','4.1.2.1'),
q(2,'Give one reliability advantage of tides over wind and one limitation of tidal generation.','- Tidal timing is **predictable**, whereas wind varies unpredictably.\n- Tidal output is **not continuous**, so other supply or storage may still be needed.','4.1.3'),
q(3,'Calculate the kinetic energy of a 1,200 kg car travelling at 15 m/s.','- **Eₖ = ½mv²**.\n- Eₖ = 0.5 × 1,200 × 15².\n- **Eₖ = 135,000 J**.','4.1.1.2'),
q(3,'A 3.0 kg load is lifted through 2.0 m. Use g = 9.8 N/kg to calculate its increase in gravitational potential energy.','- **Eₚ = mgΔh**.\n- Eₚ = 3.0 × 9.8 × 2.0.\n- **58.8 J** (about 59 J).','4.1.1.2'),
q(3,'A spring of constant 160 N/m is stretched by 5.0 cm within its proportional limit. Calculate the energy stored.','- Convert e = **0.050 m**.\n- **Eₑ = ½ke²** = 0.5 × 160 × 0.050².\n- **0.20 J**.','4.1.1.2'),
q(3,'A 0.80 kg sample warms from 18°C to 43°C. Its specific heat capacity is 900 J/kg°C. Calculate the thermal energy increase.','- Δθ = 43 − 18 = **25°C**.\n- **ΔE = mcΔθ** = 0.80 × 900 × 25.\n- **18,000 J**.','4.1.1.3','2022 1H Q04.4; 2023 1H Q02'),
q(3,'Explain why lubricating moving machine parts can increase efficiency.','- Lubrication **reduces friction**.\n- Less input energy transfers to unwanted **thermal stores**.\n- A greater fraction of input becomes the **useful output**.','4.1.2.2','2022 1H Q01.3'),
q(3,'A heater transfers 9,000 J to a 0.50 kg block. Its temperature rises by 20°C. Calculate the specific heat capacity, assuming all energy heats the block.','- **c = E/(mΔθ)**.\n- c = 9,000/(0.50 × 20).\n- **900 J/kg°C**.','4.1.1.3','2023 1H Q02'),
q(3,'A metal-block heating experiment loses energy to the room. Explain the effect on the calculated specific heat capacity if all electrical input is treated as energy gained by the block.','- The measured temperature rise is **smaller** than if all input heated the block.\n- The calculation uses **c = E/(mΔθ)** with an input E that includes losses.\n- The calculated c is therefore **too high**. Insulation reduces this error.','4.1.1.3','2023 1H Q02.3'),
q(4,'A motor of efficiency 0.70 lifts a 20 kg load through 3.0 m in 5.0 s. Use g = 10 N/kg. Calculate the useful power and the total electrical input power.','- Useful energy = mgΔh = 20 × 10 × 3.0 = **600 J**.\n- Useful power = E/t = 600/5.0 = **120 W**.\n- Efficiency = useful power / input power.\n- Input power = 120/0.70 = **171 W** (about 170 W).','4.1.1.2–4.1.2.2'),
q(4,'Two equal-mass samples receive equal energy from identical heaters. A warms by 12°C and B by 30°C. Which has the larger specific heat capacity, and by what factor?','- **c = E/(mΔθ)**.\n- With equal E and m, c is **inversely proportional to temperature rise**.\n- A has the larger c because it warms less.\n- cA/cB = 30/12 = **2.5**.','4.1.1.3'),
q(4,'A 2.0 kg object falls 5.0 m from rest. Air resistance transfers 18 J to thermal stores. Use g = 9.8 N/kg. Calculate its final speed.','- Gravitational energy lost = 2.0 × 9.8 × 5.0 = **98 J**.\n- Kinetic energy gained = 98 − 18 = **80 J**.\n- v = **√(2Eₖ/m)** = √(160/2).\n- **v = 8.94 m/s**.','4.1.1.2–4.1.2.1'),
q(6,'Describe how to measure the specific heat capacity of a metal block using an electrical heater. Include measurements, a calculation and ways to improve the result.','- Measure the block’s **mass** with a balance. Fit a heater and temperature probe, using suitable oil to improve contact.\n- Wrap the block in **insulation** and record the initial temperature. Use a low-voltage supply.\n- Measure the energy delivered using a joulemeter, or record V, I and heating time and calculate **E = VIt**.\n- Record temperatures regularly and find **Δθ** for the measured input energy.\n- Calculate **c = E/(mΔθ)** and repeat to assess consistency.\n- Insulation reduces losses to the room; good contact reduces lag. Some energy also heats equipment, so using all input can overestimate c. Avoid hot surfaces and keep electrical connections dry.','4.1.1.3','2023 1H Q02: related practical and calculation skills'),
q(6,'Plan an investigation comparing three materials as thermal insulators around containers of hot water.','- Use identical containers with **equal thicknesses** of the three materials and one uninsulated control.\n- Add equal masses of water at the **same starting temperature** and fit matching lids.\n- Keep room conditions and exposed area the same; use the same thermometer arrangement.\n- Measure temperature at fixed intervals for a fixed total time. Handle hot water safely on a stable surface.\n- Repeat and calculate mean temperature drops; plot cooling curves.\n- The smallest mean drop over the same interval indicates the best insulator in this setup. If repeat ranges overlap strongly, collect more data before claiming a clear difference.','4.1.2.1','2022 1H Q04'),
q(6,'A town wants a reliable electricity supply with lower carbon emissions. Evaluate replacing all gas generation with wind turbines.','- Wind is **renewable** and does not burn fuel during operation, reducing operational CO₂ compared with gas.\n- However, wind speed varies and turbines may stop in very weak or very strong winds, so installed maximum power is not guaranteed output.\n- Storage, connections to other regions or controllable backup would help match demand; these have costs and limitations.\n- Turbines can affect views, noise and wildlife; construction and maintenance also have environmental costs. Gas is controllable but releases CO₂ and uses finite fuel.\n- A mixed supply with wind, storage and other reliable low-carbon sources is generally more defensible than assuming wind alone can meet every demand. The final choice should use local output, cost and site data.','4.1.3','2022 1H Q01.2–01.4')]
write_bank(1,items,'Energy equations and efficiency appear in both papers (2022 Q03–04; 2023 Q01–02). Heating/practical reasoning also appears in both (2022 Q04; 2023 Q02). Resource reliability and lubrication are illustrated by 2022 Q01; store-transfer explanations by 2023 Q07.2. Each broad type is counted once per paper; categories overlap. Questions here use new wording, values and contexts.')
